  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a smart lock work?",
      "a": "A small motor slides the bolt, and a chip decides when to run it. The chip listens for a coded message from your phone, a keypad or an app server and unlocks only if the code checks out.",
      "asks": [
        "What is inside a smart lock?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a smart lock know it is my phone?",
      "a": "When you set it up, the lock and your phone share secret keys. Each unlock message is encrypted with those keys, so the lock can tell it is genuine and not copied.",
      "asks": [
        "Can a smart lock be hacked?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens to a smart lock if the battery dies?",
      "a": "The lock warns you weeks ahead. If the battery dies anyway, most locks have a backup key or let you touch a battery to contacts outside to power it briefly.",
      "asks": [
        "Will I be locked out if the battery runs out?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can a smart lock be hacked?",
      "a": "Good smart locks use strong encryption, which is very hard to break, but cheap ones have had security flaws. Keeping the app updated and using a strong password helps.",
      "asks": [
        "Are smart locks safe?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a smart lock unlock when I arrive?",
      "a": "Some use your phone’s location to notice you are near home, then unlock over Bluetooth when you are right at the door.",
      "asks": [
        "What is auto-unlock?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Bluetooth used by smart locks typically works over a range of about 10 metres.",
    "Smart locks run on batteries that usually last several months, and warn the owner when they are low."
  ],
